[38;2;124;124;124mNow the proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConstruction of the Cantor set: Start with [0,1]. At step n, remove the open [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmiddle third of each remaining interval. The Cantor set C is the intersection of[0m[38;2;124;124;124mall remaining sets C_n. Each C_n is a union of 2^n closed intervals each of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlength 3^{-n}, so m(C_n) = (2/3)^n. Thus m(C) = lim (2/3)^n = 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConstruction of the Cantor function: Define f on [0,1]. At step n, on the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mremoved middle thirds, define f to be constant. Specifically:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- On [1/3, 2/3], f = 1/2.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- On [1/9, 2/9], f = 1/4; on [7/9, 8/9], f = 3/4.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Generally, on each removed interval at step n, f takes the value k/2^n for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mappropriate k.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOn the Cantor set C itself, define f(x) = sup{f(y) : y ∈ complement, y ≤ x} or [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mequivalently via the ternary expansion.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Cantor function is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Continuous (it's uniformly continuous, monotone, and the jumps would only [0m
[38;2;124;124;124moccur at removed intervals but we set it constant there; actually one shows [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontinuity by noting the oscillation on C_n is at most 2^{-n} → 0).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Non-decreasing.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- f(C) = [0,1]: Every value in [0,1] is achieved. Indeed, points in C have [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mternary expansions using only digits 0 and 2. Map such a point x = Σ a_n 3^{-n} [0m[38;2;124;124;124m(a_n ∈ {0,2}) to f(x) = Σ (a_n/2) 2^{-n}, which is a binary expansion covering [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mall of [0,1]. So f maps C onto [0,1].[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore m(f(C)) = m([0,1]) = 1 > 0 = m(C).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis shows such a continuous function exists.[0m

